  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'State the reasons for weighing the medical wastes generated at hospitals in Bhutan before they are discarded. (3 marks)', 'Healthcare waste is weighed and recorded before disposal because:
1. Monitoring and records - the hospital and the Ministry of Health need data on how much infectious, sharp, pharmaceutical and general waste each ward or facility produces, to follow the national healthcare waste management guidelines and report it.
2. Planning and cost - the weight decides the number of bins and bags, storage space, transport and the capacity of treatment equipment (autoclave, incinerator, deep burial pits), and helps estimate disposal costs.
3. Safety, segregation and accountability - sudden changes show poor segregation or overuse, help reduce waste, and make sure hazardous items (sharps, expired drugs) are fully accounted for and not reused or stolen.', null, '[{"point": "Record keeping and monitoring: the amount of each category of waste (infectious, sharps, pharmaceutical, general) generated per ward/day, as required by national healthcare waste guidelines", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Planning and costing: to plan containers, storage, transport and treatment capacity (autoclave, incinerator) and to budget/cost disposal", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Safety and accountability: to check segregation and reduce waste, detect misuse or theft (e.g. syringes, drugs) and report to the Ministry/regulators", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
