  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Bhutan''s constitution mandates a minimum of 60% forest cover, and the country''s forest cover is reported to have increased from 71% to over 80%. Explain the positive and negative implications of this in terms of the socioeconomic development of the country. (3 marks)', 'Positive implications:
• Environment: Bhutan remains carbon-negative, with clean air and water and protected watersheds.
• Economy: forests protect the rivers that feed hydropower, Bhutan''s largest export; they support high-value low-volume tourism and ecotourism, provide timber and non-wood forest products (mushrooms, bamboo, medicinal plants), and attract climate finance and international goodwill.
• Biodiversity and culture are conserved.
Negative implications:
• Less land is available for agriculture, roads, hydropower and industrial estates, housing and urban growth, raising costs and slowing some projects (land-use restrictions, lengthy forestry clearances).
• Human-wildlife conflict increases - wild boar, monkeys and elephants damage crops and predators kill livestock, adding to rural poverty, fallow land and migration to towns.
• Large forest areas also raise the risk of forest fires and require resources to manage.', null, '[{"point": "Positive: carbon-negative status, clean air/water, watershed protection for hydropower (main export earner)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Positive: biodiversity and ecotourism, non-wood forest products and timber, international goodwill and climate finance", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Negative: less land for farming, roads, industry and housing; higher construction costs and restrictions on land use", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Negative: more human-wildlife conflict (crop damage, livestock loss), forest-fire risk and rural-urban migration/food insecurity", "marks": 0.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
