  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Classify carbohydrates based on their structure and composition and give at least three examples. (3 marks)', 'Carbohydrates are classified by the number of sugar units:
1. Monosaccharides - simple sugars that cannot be hydrolysed further, e.g. glucose, fructose, galactose, ribose.
2. Disaccharides - two monosaccharides joined by a glycosidic bond, e.g. sucrose (glucose + fructose), lactose (glucose + galactose), maltose (glucose + glucose). (Oligosaccharides have 3-10 units, e.g. raffinose.)
3. Polysaccharides - long chains of many units, e.g. starch (plant storage), glycogen (animal storage), cellulose (plant cell walls).', null, '[{"point": "Monosaccharides - single sugar units, e.g. glucose, fructose, galactose", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Disaccharides (and oligosaccharides) - 2 (2-10) units, e.g. sucrose, lactose, maltose", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Polysaccharides - many units, e.g. starch, glycogen, cellulose", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
