  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What are iodine deficiency disorders (IDD)? Describe their effects and explain how Bhutan has controlled them. (3 marks)', 'Iodine deficiency disorders are the range of health problems caused when the diet lacks iodine, which the thyroid gland needs to make the hormones thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3).
Effects: enlargement of the thyroid (goitre); hypothyroidism with tiredness and weight gain; in pregnancy - miscarriage, stillbirth and babies born with cretinism (severe mental and physical retardation); in children - lower IQ, poor growth and poor school performance; deaf-mutism.
Control in Bhutan: universal iodisation of salt (iodised salt produced at the Bhutan Salt Industry in Phuentsholing and only iodised salt allowed for sale), regular monitoring of salt iodine content and of urinary iodine in school children, and public education. With these measures Bhutan was recognised in 2003 as one of the first countries to achieve sustainable elimination of IDD.', null, '[{"point": "IDD: health problems caused by too little dietary iodine, which the thyroid needs to make thyroxine (T3, T4)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Effects: goitre, hypothyroidism, cretinism and mental retardation in children of deficient mothers, stillbirths/miscarriage, poor school performance", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Control: universal salt iodisation (iodised salt law and Bhutan Salt Industry), monitoring of salt and urinary iodine, awareness; Bhutan was among the first countries declared to have eliminated IDD (2003)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
