  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain the principle of the autoclave and state the standard conditions used to sterilise hospital instruments. (3 marks)', 'Principle: an autoclave sterilises by moist heat. Water is heated in a sealed chamber so the steam is under pressure; at higher pressure water boils at a higher temperature. Saturated steam at this temperature penetrates materials and kills all microorganisms, including bacterial spores, by coagulating and denaturing their proteins.
Standard conditions: 121 °C at a pressure of about 15 lb per square inch (about 1 bar above atmospheric) for 15-20 minutes; or 134 °C for 3-5 minutes in high-speed cycles.
Use and checks: used for surgical instruments, dressings, linen, culture media and infectious laboratory waste. Items must be cleaned and loosely packed so steam can reach every surface, air must be removed, and each cycle is checked with indicator tape and periodically with biological indicators (spores of Geobacillus stearothermophilus).', null, '[{"point": "Principle: moist heat - saturated steam under pressure reaches a higher temperature than boiling water and kills all microbes including spores by denaturing/coagulating proteins", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Standard conditions: 121 °C at about 15 psi (1 bar above atmospheric) for 15-20 minutes (or 134 °C for 3-5 minutes)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Use and checks: for instruments, linen, culture media and infectious waste; correct loading, air removal; chemical indicator tape/biological indicator (Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores) to confirm sterilisation", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
